# Firebase Admin Service Account & Realtime DB URL
# You can supply the Firebase service account to the app in one of the following
# secure ways. Pick ONE method — do NOT commit service account JSON to git.
#
# 1) Provide the service account file path relative to the project root:
#    FIREBASE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT=./firebase-service-account.json
#    (Not recommended for public repos — use only for local/dev builds.)
#
# 2) Provide the raw JSON content of the service account as the env value:
#    FIREBASE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT={"type":"service_account","project_id":...}
#    This requires your host to support multiline env values or secret storage.
#
# 3) Provide the BASE64-encoded JSON as the env value (single-line safe):
#    FIREBASE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT=eyJ0eXBlIjoi... (base64 of JSON)
#    The app will detect and decode base64 automatically.
#
# 4) Provide a path on the host/container where the secret is mounted:
#    FIREBASE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_PATH=/run/secrets/firebase-sa.json
#    (Recommended for Docker Swarm, Kubernetes secrets, or platform secret mounts.)
#
# The application attempts these, in order:
#  - parse FIREBASE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT as JSON
#  - parse FIREBASE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT as base64-encoded JSON
#  - treat FIREBASE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT as a relative path (project root)
#  - if FIREBASE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_PATH is set, use it as an absolute path
#
# For deployed platforms (Render, Heroku, Vercel, Docker):
# - Add the secret via the platform's secret/ENV mechanism (base64 or raw JSON).
# - If you must mount a file, set FIREBASE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_PATH to the mounted path.
# - Keep `.env` and service account JSON out of the image if possible; use secret mounts.

FIREBASE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT=
FIREBASE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_PATH=



